@@ -0,0 +1,233 @@
+# Scene-Referred Content
+
+What changes for the person building a level once the Cinematic Camera's **Scene Referred**
+property is on, what ships with the plugin to make that easier, and the two procedures — the
+physical time-of-day preset and the probe bake — that a ReC scene needs.
+
+The engine's own path is untouched. With **Scene Referred** off the renderer is byte for byte the
+stock engine. Everything below is about the other path.
+
+---
+
+## 1. The one thing to know first
+
+**A level looks different with the camera on, and that is by design.** Not a bug, not a
+regression, and not something a setting will make go away.
+
+The reason is units. CRYENGINE's sun is divided by pi on the light side to make up for a `1/pi`
+that its diffuse and specular lobes both omit, while local lights are left alone. The result is
+that a point light is 1.65 stops brighter, relative to the sun, than physics says it should be —
+which is why the shipped defaults put the sun only 1.9 stops above a default point light when the
+honest ratio is 3.6. The scene-referred path fixes it at the source: every light is radiometric
+and the `1/pi` goes back into the BRDF where it belongs.
+
+What that means for content already in your level:
+
+| Source | On the scene-referred path |
+|---|---|
+| Sun | unchanged |
+| Point, projector, area light | **1.65 stops darker** |
+| Ambient light (`DLF_AMBIENT`) | unchanged (it is a tint, not a radiance) |
+| Environment probe | unchanged by the convention, but its *contents* are now stale — see section 4 |
+| Emissive material | unchanged |
+| Skybox with an HDR texture | **3.32 stops darker**, and now delivers exactly its authored lux |
+| Sun shafts | now follow the sun, the aperture and ND instead of sitting at a fixed display fraction |
+
+So a sun-lit exterior gets more contrast between sun and shade, and a point-lit interior drops
+1.65 stops against its sky-derived ambient. **ReC scenes are lit for the ReC path.** Re-lighting
+an existing level means raising local lights by about 1.65 stops (a factor of pi) if you want the
+old balance back — but the point of the exercise is that you probably do not, because the old
+balance was not a real one.
+
+Two more, less obvious:
+
+* **Area lights are not corrected yet.** They go through an LTC integrator that never calls the
+  shared specular BRDF, so they do not receive the `1/pi` and are now 1.65 stops hot relative to
+  point lights. Whether the LTC lookup already carries part of that factor has to be measured
+  before it is fixed; until then, treat area lights as needing a manual trim.
+* **Legacy `.tod` files are path-dependent.** A pre-3.8.2 time-of-day file is converted to lux at
+  load time, and that conversion has to know which convention it is converting *into*. Loading
+  happens before any camera has activated, so a legacy `.tod` opened in a normal session and then
+  viewed on the scene-referred path reads pi times (1.65 stops) too bright. The fix is to re-save
+  the preset with the camera active, or simply to author a `.env` — the legacy format has been
+  dead since CE 3.8.2 and only `Editor/asset_thumbnail.tod` still uses it.
+
+---
+
+## 2. The physical preset: `ReC_Physical.env`
+
+Ships at `Code/CryPlugins/CinematicCamera/Assets/Environment/ReC_Physical.env` together with its
+`.env.cryasset` sidecar.
+
+### Installing it
+
+Copy **both** files into your project's asset directory — `<project>/libs/environmentpresets/` is
+where the engine looks for presets and where the Level Editor's *Environment Presets* panel adds
+them from. Then, in the Level Editor, open **Level Settings → Environment Presets**, add
+`ReC_Physical`, and make it the default for the level. The Asset Browser will also show it as an
+Environment asset once the sidecar is in the project.
+
+Do **not** copy it over `Engine/EngineAssets/Shading/default.env`. That file is the template every
+new level in the engine is created from; overwriting it would change the stock path for every
+project on the machine.
+
+### What is in it
+
+Everything is a real photometric number, and every range that needed widening is widened **in the
+file**, not in code — an `.env` stores its own min/max per variable, so a preset can carry a wider
+range without touching anything shared.
+
+| Variable | Value | Range in the file | Why |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Sun intensity (lux) | 100 000 at midday, 90 000 at 09:00/15:00, 1 000 at 06:00/18:00, 0 at night | 0 … 550 000 | clear-sky direct-normal illuminance peaks near 120 000 lux; 100 000 is a normal bright day |
+| Sun colour | 1.00, 0.96, 0.92 at midday; warmer at the horizon | 0 … 1 (forced) | colour temperature only — all scale lives in the intensity |
+| Skybox intensity (lux) | 20 000 at midday | 0 … **200 000** (stock caps at 20 000) | clear-sky diffuse illuminance on a horizontal surface. The stock default sat at 99 % of its own ceiling, which is why the range is widened. |
+| Skybox colour / filter / opacity | white, 1, 1 | | so an HDR sky texture, if you assign one, is used unmodified |
+| Sky Light sun intensity multiplier | 50 | 0 … 1000 | the procedural dome. **Uncalibrated** — see section 3. |
+| Night sky horizon / zenith multipliers | 2e-6 / 1e-6 | 0 … 0.1 | a real moonlit night sky is a few thousandths of a cd/m². The stock defaults are about twenty times brighter than that. |
+| Night sky moon / corona multipliers | 1e-2 / 1e-5 / 5e-6 | 0 … 0.1 | the moon disc stays the brightest thing in a night sky, as it should |
+| HDR EV min / max | −4 / 20 | −10 … 20 | the engine's own exposure window. Inert while the camera owns exposure, but widened so it can never clamp anything. |
+| HDR EV auto compensation | 0 | −5 … 5 | the camera does its own compensation |
+| HDR dynamic power factor | 0 | | deprecated, and non-zero it silently scales fog and the night sky |
+| Fog colour multiplier | 0 | 0 … 16 | fog is a separate stage (S8); it starts off |
+
+Skybox "Intensity (lux)" means **illuminance on a horizontal surface**, which is what a light
+meter pointed at the ground reads and what a DP would author. Internally the engine converts it to
+sky luminance with `E = pi * L`; that divide is correct and stays on both paths. What was wrong —
+and is fixed on the scene-referred path — is a separate factor of ten that made a textured skybox
+deliver ten times its authored lux.
+
+The preset is saved as preset **version 3**, the same version the shipped `default.env` uses. That
+version's loader matches variables by id and ignores the rest, so the preset only has to carry the
+variables it actually changes; everything else keeps the engine's own default.
+
+### Exposing it — read this before you decide the preset is broken
+
+The camera's shipped Manual settings are **T5.6, 1/50, ISO 400**, which is
+
+```
+EV100 = log2(T^2 / t) - log2(ISO/100) = log2(31.36 * 50) - log2(4) = 8.615
+```
+
+and a saturation luminance of `L_sat = 1.2 * 2^8.615 = 470 cd/m^2`. That is an *interior* exposure.
+Physical midday daylight is around **EV100 14.8**. The two are 6.2 stops apart, and no amount of
+authoring can close that gap without making the preset lie about its own units.
+
+The gap is closed the way it is closed on a real set: **set `ND Filter (stops)` to 6** on the
+camera's Lens group. Then
+
+```
+EV100 = 8.615 + 6 = 14.615      L_sat = 1.2 * 2^14.615 = 30 100 cd/m^2
+E on a sunlit horizontal surface = 100 000 * cos(30 deg) + 20 000 = 106 600 lux
+L of an 18 % Lambertian surface  = 0.18 * 106 600 / pi = 6 108 cd/m^2
+exposed value                     = 6 108 / 30 100 = 0.203
+```
+
+which is within a quarter stop of the 0.18 the whole pipeline is anchored on. Trim with the
+aperture, the shutter or exposure compensation to taste; ND 6 is the coarse dial that puts you in
+the right decade. Or switch the camera to **AUTO** and let it meter, which is what it is for.
+
+> **Documented deviation.** The S2 task asked for a preset that "opens looking right at the
+> camera's default Manual settings" *and* for a sun of ~100 000 lux. Those two are the 6.2 stops
+> above; they cannot both be true. The physical values were kept, because D1 makes real light
+> ratios the point of the exercise and the sun/shade acceptance test needs them, and the ND became
+> the single documented dial. If you would rather the preset open flat at the shipped defaults,
+> scale the sun and skybox curves down by 6 stops (a factor of 64) — but then the preset is no
+> longer physical and the numbers in the panel stop meaning lux.
+
+### The sun/shade ratio
+
+The S2 acceptance asks for at least 10 stops between sun and shade in a ReC scene. That ratio is a
+property of the **geometry**, not of the preset: an 18 % surface in open shade under the full sky
+still receives 20 000 lux, only 2.4 stops below the same surface in sun. Ten stops appears where
+the sky is occluded too — inside a doorway, under a vehicle, in a room lit through one window.
+What the preset guarantees is that both ends of that range are real numbers; the level provides
+the occlusion.
+
+---
+
+## 3. What the preset cannot fix on its own
+
+**The procedural (Nishita) sky is uncalibrated.** It is a third brightness system with no
+documented unit and no relationship to the photometric one that can be read out of the source. If
+your level uses the procedural dome — which it does whenever the sky material has no diffuse
+texture — its absolute brightness is a guess. Measure it and set `r_SceneReferredNishitaScale`;
+the procedure is in `SceneReferredCalibration.md` section 2.
+
+**Sun shaft strength changed meaning.** On the scene-referred path the shafts carry the sun's real
+exposed radiance instead of a normalised hue, so the amount is now a scattering coefficient and
+its useful value is of order 0.01–0.05 rather than 0.25. Calibration procedure in
+`SceneReferredCalibration.md` section 3.
+
+**Volumetric fog, volumetric clouds, SVOGI and the stock LDR post stage** are not yet on scene
+units. They are deliberately left running and visibly wrong rather than switched off, so that the
+breakage is in front of you and gets fixed rather than hidden. They are Stage S8.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Re-baking environment probes
+
+**Every probe in a ReC scene has to be re-baked.** A probe is a photograph of the scene's lighting,
+and the scene's lighting has moved: local lights are 1.65 stops down and a textured sky is 3.32
+stops down. An old probe is a picture of a level that no longer exists.
+
+### The convention stamp
+
+A probe baked under the scene-referred convention is **byte-indistinguishable** from a stock one.
+The `.dds` has no version field, the resource compiler has nowhere to put one, and the two can
+differ by up to 3.3 stops. So the stamp is the filename: ReC bakes are written as
+`<name>_rec_cm.tif` (and `_rec_cm.dds` after compilation), and the bake path adds the `_rec` for
+you whenever the camera's Scene Referred property is on.
+
+At load, a probe whose name disagrees with the convention the frame is being lit under logs one
+warning naming the probe. If you see
+
+```
+Environment probe 'Probe_Hall': cube map '...' was baked under the stock light-unit convention
+but the frame is lit under the scene-referred one - it is up to 3.3 stops wrong.
+```
+
+the probe needs re-baking, not tuning.
+
+### Procedure
+
+1. Open the level in Sandbox with the Cinematic Camera present and its **Scene Referred** property
+   **ON**. The bake inherits the 3D engine's convention from the live switch, so this is the step
+   that decides which convention the asset carries.
+2. Set the time of day you are baking for. Probes are baked from one moment.
+3. Select the probes and press **Generate** (or *Generate to default path*). Each writes a 16x
+   supersampled fp16 cube map, calls the resource compiler, and reloads the result.
+4. Check the filenames: they should now carry `_rec`. The probe entity is re-pointed at the file
+   that was actually written.
+5. Reload the level and confirm no convention warnings appear.
+
+Notes that matter:
+
+* **The bake is taken at unity exposure, automatically.** Cube-map generation forces the camera's
+  pre-exposure to 1.0, so the cube map stores unexposed radiance and the runtime tint multiply
+  exposes it exactly once. Without that the exposure would be squared, and BC6H is unsigned so
+  nothing could carry a compensation back in. Nothing to do; just do not be surprised that the
+  bake ignores your aperture.
+* **The bake is tone-map-free and post-free** — that is stock behaviour and it is correct.
+* **Probes are pinned to 256x256 BC6UH.** The atlas is a texture array, so every slice must be the
+  same size. Raising the resolution is not a per-probe decision.
+* **`r_HideSunInCubemaps` only hides the *procedural* sun.** If your sky is an HDR texture with a
+  painted sun, that sun is in the probe, and it will show up as a bright locked highlight on every
+  reflective surface in the probe's volume. Arguably correct for a scene-referred pipeline; decide
+  deliberately rather than being surprised.
+* **The editor's "generate all cubemaps" script does not cover component probes.** Select them and
+  bake them; a re-bake that silently skips half the probes is worse than none.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Quick reference
+
+| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
+|---|---|---|
+| Whole level blows out when Scene Referred goes on | camera at EV 8.6, scene at EV 14.8 | ND Filter 6, or AUTO |
+| Interiors went dark relative to the sky | local lights are 1.65 stops down by design | re-light, or raise them by a factor of pi |
+| Reflections and ambient do not match direct light | probes are stale | re-bake (section 4) |
+| Warning about a probe's light-unit convention | probe baked under the other convention | re-bake with the switch in the state you ship |
+| Sky far too bright / far too dark relative to the sun | procedural dome uncalibrated | `r_SceneReferredNishitaScale`, `SceneReferredCalibration.md` section 2 |
+| Sun shafts washing the frame out | amount is now a scattering coefficient | drop it to 0.01–0.05 |
+| A legacy `.tod` reads 1.65 stops bright | migration is path-dependent | re-save the preset with the camera active, or author a `.env` |
